      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We aimed to evaluate the association between eosinophilic inflammation in induced sputum and pulmonary function and persistent airflow limitation in children."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A total of 92 asthmatic children and 72 controls were enrolled in this study. Eosinophil count (%) and eosinophil cationic protein (ECP) levels were measured in induced sputum. We performed spirometry and a methacholine challenge test, and measured total eosinophil count, total serum IgE, and serum ECP in all subjects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Asthmatic children had significantly higher levels of sputum eosinophils (9% vs. 0%; P < 0.001) and sputum ECP (2.3 ± 0.7 vs. 1.6 ± 0.6 log µg/L, p < .001) than controls. Sputum ECP levels showed a significant negative correlation with post-bronchodilator (post-BD) FEV(1) (r = -0.307; p = .001) and post-BD FEV(1)/FVC (r = -0.286; p = .002), whereas sputum eosinophils showed no correlation with post-BD FEV(1) and post-BD FEV(1)/FVC. However, no significant differences in sputum ECP and sputum eosinophil counts were observed in asthmatic children with and without persistent airflow limitation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our findings suggest that sputum eosinophilic inflammation, especially ECP, is associated with pulmonary function and persistent airflow limitation, which is manifested by low post-BD FEV(1)/FVC."
        }
      ]},
